  ],
  "slug": "ai-topic-c4ec",
  "summary": "Recent preprints dissect the structural limits of biological foundation models, from DNA readout design to perturbation decoder rank and LLM evidence use.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is diagnostic scrutiny of biological models: asking which structural or evaluative property limits performance rather than reporting accuracy alone. An audit of the STATE perturbation decoder attributes reliability limits to a low-rank attention bottleneck, placing a rank-based ceiling on predictions. BEND 2.0 extends DNA language model evaluation from variant effect prediction to sequence design, reporting linear readouts and post-training as improvements. An auditable agentic LLM pipeline for GWAS variant-to-gene prioritisation finds that reranking gains track known-gene recall rather than evidence use, exposing a gap between apparent and grounded performance. CellCanon proposes an agentic single-cell world model that unifies expression prediction, evidence-grounded explanation, and observation-driven updating. What is new is the explicit identification of architectural and evaluative ceilings, and of whether models actually use the evidence they cite.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-07"
}
